"note": "Joseph Simeon Hinds's father — named in the family links on his memorial (Find A Grave #81557074). Joseph Simeon's biography records him buying “shares of his father's lands from his brothers, Simeon and Levi” when the family moved to Guilford County, North Carolina about 1774. The published Hinds family history (Ancestry) records this family's English tradition: its entry reads “Joseph Hinds, (parentage unknown) is supposed to have come from England… Child: Simeon” — its Simeon is this line's Capt. Simeon Hinds (m. Elizabeth Stone, Tennessee). The book's “Joseph” most likely stands for Joseph Simeon Hinds (1737); the origin stays “supposed,” as the book itself hedges it. Parents per 2 membe HINDS 2026-10-15 (round 43): memorial dates — died Aug 1772 in Guilford Co., N.C. (Find A Grave #240348730). The 1770 Mendham Township, N.J. reconstructed-census entry records him on a 12 Mar 1771 petition to the governor about a fire at Henry Gillam's house; the 1761 Rowan Co., N.C. entry is recorded as a lead. The 1956 SAR application (no. 81305) files him with spouse Ruth and child John Hinds — a filed lineage for a son not otherwise sourced; recorded as a lead.\n\nROUND 48 (2026-10-15): a wider Public Member Trees read (name with the 1700-1710 birth window, three result pages) still shows no two trees agreeing on his parents. The parent claims sitting on those pages are Thomas Hinds (1645-1720), Thomas Hinds (1742-1826), a James Hinds / Heynes / Haynes / Haines (1647-1719) in several spellings, and a same-name elder Joseph Hinds (1647-1719). The same pages carry unrelated men of the name from Maryland, Dorset and Nottinghamshire, so the rows cannot be read as an agreement without opening each tree; nothing is wired and the rung stays open.",
